            "order": 1,
            "notes": "# Module 1: Introduction to DBMS & Relational Model\n\n---\n\n## 1.1 What is Data?\n\n# 1.1 What is Data?\n\n### What is Data?\nData is a collection of raw, unorganized facts, figures, symbols, or observations that can be processed to produce meaningful information. In computing, data is represented in binary format and structured in databases.\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 What is Database?\n\n# 1.2 What is Database?\n\n### What is a Database?\nA database is an organized collection of structured data stored electronically in a computer system. Databases are controlled by a Database Management System (DBMS).\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 DBMS Introduction\n\n# 1.3 DBMS Introduction\n\n### Introduction to DBMS\nA Database Management System (DBMS) is software that manages databases, allowing users to store, retrieve, update, and organize information efficiently while ensuring data integrity.\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 Database vs File System\n\n# 1.4 Database vs File System\n\n### Database vs File System\nUnlike traditional file systems, a DBMS handles data redundancy, concurrency control, security, data integrity, and complex queries seamlessly.\n\n---\n\n## 1.5 Advantages of DBMS\n\n# 1.5 Advantages of DBMS\n\n## Advantages of DBMS\nKey benefits include: minimized data redundancy, data sharing, data consistency, transactional safety, secure access, and backup & recovery services.\n\n---\n\n## 1.6 Types of Databases\n\n# 1.6 Types of Databases\n\n### Types of Databases\nDatabases are categorized into Relational (RDBMS), NoSQL (Key-Value, Document, Graph), Distributed, Cloud, and Object-Oriented databases.",
