            "courseId": "database-management-system",
            "order": 1,
            "notes": "# Module 4: SQL Joins, Subqueries & Set Operations\n\n---\n\n## 4.1 GROUP BY\n\n# 4.1 GROUP BY\n\n### GROUP BY Clause\nGroups rows sharing identical values: `SELECT category, COUNT(*) FROM products GROUP BY category;`\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 HAVING\n\n# 4.2 HAVING\n\n### HAVING Clause\nFilters group results (unlike WHERE which filters rows): `SELECT category FROM products GROUP BY category HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;`\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 JOINS\n\n# 4.3 JOINS\n\n### SQL JOINS\nCombines columns from multiple tables. Types: INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, FULL OUTER JOIN.\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 UNION\n\n# 4.4 UNION\n\n### UNION Operator\nCombines query results into one distinct list: `SELECT id FROM customers UNION SELECT id FROM employees;`\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 Subqueries\n\n# 4.5 Subqueries\n\n### Subqueries\nQueries nested inside other queries: `SELECT * FROM products WHERE price > (SELECT AVG(price) FROM products);`\n\n---\n\n## 4.6 Views\n\n# 4.6 Views\n\n### Database Views\nA virtual table built from a SELECT statement: `CREATE VIEW active_users AS SELECT * FROM users WHERE active = true;`\n\n---\n\n## 4.7 Indexes\n\n# 4.7 Indexes\n\n### Indexes\nStructures that speed up query execution: `CREATE INDEX idx_name ON users(name);`",
